     "text": "The table treats **A0 as the context function**, as requested, with **A1\u2013A4** as its functional decomposition. Inputs are transformed information; controls govern the transformation; mechanisms perform it. The child functions describe responsibilities, not sequential program steps.\n\n| Node | Function | Inputs | Controls | Outputs | Mechanisms |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **A0** | **Control tank level** | StartCommand signal (%IX100.0); StopCommand signal (%IX100.1); LevelMeter reading (%IW100) | I/O mapping and scaling specification; FULL_SCALE = 1000; measuring span h_span = 10 V; set point h_sp = h_target = 0.8 h_span; overfill limit h_max = 0.9 h_span; tolerance eps = 0.02 h_span; hold time t_h = 10 s; PID gains Kp, Ti, Td; initial-step, start-event, stop-priority, completion, output-clamping, and integration-suspension rules; discharge opening specification of 40% | FillValve command (%QW100); DischargeValve command (%QW101); fill_valve; discharge_valve | OpenPLC Runtime; PLC control program; scan clock and retained state; Modbus TCP connection and Factory I/O driver; Factory I/O level meter and valve interfaces |\n| **A1** | **Derive command events and level conditions** | StartCommand signal; StopCommand signal; LevelMeter reading | Scaling specification; FULL_SCALE; h_span; h_target; h_max; eps; t_h; regulation-state information; start-edge detection rule; continuous-tolerance timing and timer-reset rules | start; stop; measured level h; level error e; settled; r | Input acquisition and scaling logic; rising-edge detector and previous-input memory; comparison logic; hold timer; PLC scan clock |\n| **A2** | **Manage regulation state** | start; stop; settled; r | Initial step 0; start-event acceptance in step 0; stop priority; regulation termination rule: settled or r = HIGH; inactive-state output prohibition | Regulation-state information; fill_valve; discharge_valve | State-transition logic; retained step/state memory; OpenPLC Runtime |\n| **A3** | **Calculate bounded PID fill demand** | Level error e | fill_valve; PID gains Kp, Ti, Td; PID execution interval; output limits of 0\u2013100%; integration-suspension rule while the PID output is clamped | Bounded fill-opening demand | PID algorithm; integral and derivative state memory; PLC scan clock; output limiter and integration-inhibition logic |\n| **A4** | **Generate valve output commands** | Bounded fill-opening demand | fill_valve; discharge_valve; FULL_SCALE = 1000; discharge opening specification of 40%; inactive-state zero-output rule; output register mapping | FillValve command (%QW100); DischargeValve command (%QW101) | Output scaling and integer conversion logic; output-selection logic; OpenPLC output image; Modbus TCP connection and Factory I/O driver |\n\n**Arrow meanings and operating rules**\n\n- **start** is the rising-edge event of StartCommand, TRUE for only the scan in which the button is pressed. **stop** follows the TRUE state of StopCommand.\n- **h = (LevelMeter / FULL_SCALE) \u00d7 h_span**, and **e = h_sp \u2212 h**.\n- **settled** becomes TRUE only after **|e| \u2264 eps** has remained continuously true for **10 s during regulation**. The hold timer resets when that condition is broken or regulation is inactive.\n- **r** is **LOW** for h < h_target, **MID** for h_target \u2264 h < h_max, and **HIGH** for h \u2265 h_max.\n- In step 0, **fill_valve = FALSE** and **discharge_valve = FALSE**. An accepted **start** activates regulation. During regulation, both are TRUE. **stop** has priority; **stop**, **settled**, or **r = HIGH** returns the controller to step 0, with both valve commands zero.\n- **fill_valve** and **discharge_valve** are the required Boolean activity outputs, not the analog register values. While active, FillValve receives the bounded PID demand scaled to **0\u20131000**, and DischargeValve receives **400**. Otherwise, both registers receive **0**.\n- The child-to-child links are explicit: A1 supplies conditions to A2 and error to A3; A2 supplies activity controls to A3/A4 and regulation-state feedback to A1; A3 supplies the bounded fill demand to A4.",
